index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3b93e90d7586af7c66952ca58b364cb40380019a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
+# Twin blocks for nested-dissection reduction
+
+**Status:** the generator and local gates G1--G8 pass, but this result is **not yet shippable**. The required OpenRouter oracle run was refused with HTTP 403 (`Key limit exceeded`), so G9 and the hardening verdict remain unmeasured. The error transcript is preserved rather than replaced by invented evidence.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — no-tool compression |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | graph |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (three left-vertex labels) |
+| Intended intuition | symmetry: recognize a threefold repeated incidence row |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction kind | none |
+
+## Problem and trust boundary
+
+The source is Ost, Schulz, and Strash, [*Engineering Data Reduction for Nested Dissection*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.11315). Section 2 defines open neighborhoods and elimination fill. The Twin Reduction subsection of Section 4 calls two vertices twins when their open neighborhoods agree; Theorem 4 shows that twins can be consecutive in a minimum-fill ordering, and the following exact reduction contracts one twin and later expands the ordering.
+
+An instance here is the paper's native object: a labelled 5-regular bipartite graph. The solver must output three left vertices with the same open neighborhood. `verify` checks ranges, distinctness, order, and exact equality of the three displayed neighbor sets. It neither reads `inst["answer"]` nor solves an optimization problem.
+
+The certificate is known by construction. Three provisional left vertices are chosen first. Five edge-disjoint perfect matchings are assembled so those vertices share five right neighbors while every vertex on both sides still has degree five. Both sides, the row order, and each row are then independently shuffled, carrying the witness through the relabelling.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Track A would be false. Section 4 explicitly detects twins by hashing, sorting, and comparing open neighborhoods, with stated worst-case cost `O(mn + n log n)`. On these fixed-degree rows, canonical row sorting is `O(nd log d + n log n)` and succeeds on 8/8 shipping instances. The measured run used 338,259 counted operations in total, at most 43,121 for one instance, and 0.074681 seconds wall-clock. A solver that visually recognizes the repeated row needs 13 remaining shape and set-equality checks.
+
+The relevant easy cases were not hidden: Section 3 gives linear-time perfect elimination for chordal graphs, and Section 4 gives the polynomial twin detector. This family therefore benchmarks no-tool recognition of the paper's symmetry, not complexity-theoretic hardness.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+This is the complete `demo` instance for seed 0; a person can solve it on paper by normalizing the eleven five-entry rows.
+
+```text
+Find three left vertices with identical right-neighbor sets.
+
+L6:  6 10 5 4 2
+L9:  9 3 6 10 7
+L4:  9 3 7 10 6
+L1:  5 8 1 4 0
+L7:  7 0 6 4 1
+L3:  7 9 6 3 10
+L10: 5 1 7 3 4
+L2:  9 1 3 8 2
+L0:  5 8 0 2 4
+L5:  0 8 2 1 10
+L8:  2 0 8 5 9
+
+Output exactly three zero-based left labels in increasing order.
+```
+
+The answer is `[3, 4, 9]`: all three rows encode `{3,6,7,9,10}`. `verify(inst, [3,4,9])` returns `(True, "ok")`; `verify(inst, [0,3,4])` returns `(False, "the three open neighborhoods are not equal")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | Left/right vertices | Degree | Legal triples | Ships? |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 11 + 11 | 5 | 165 | no; hand example |
+| easy | 240 + 240 | 5 | 2,275,280 | no |
+| medium | 700 + 700 | 5 | 56,921,900 | no |
+| hard | 1,600 + 1,600 | 5 | 681,387,200 | intended, pending oracle evidence |
+
+No preset has yet been rejected by an oracle or solved attack. `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` points at `hard`, but submission must wait for the required oracle and G9 files.
+
+## Gate measurements
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 planted witnesses verify; all answers JSON-native |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged answer round-trips through prose/fences; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 hits; exact density `1/681,387,200` |
+| G5 | pass | exactly one shipping answer; 16,416 failing attack candidates in 0.489562 s |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks at 0/8; reference row-sort solves 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | `n=3200` builds and verifies; answer remains three atoms |
+| G8 | pass | 40/40 key invariance and carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 16 characters, 4 estimated tokens, 3 atoms, 13 intended operations |
+| G9(a,b) | **not run** | OpenRouter key total limit returned HTTP 403 |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The bare loop made no scored attempt. All four records are API errors and therefore provide no hardness evidence, exactly as `harden.py` intends.
+
+| Preset | Seed | Model | Solved | Outcome |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 1325972988 | openai/gpt-5.6-terra | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 855273018 | x-ai/grok-4.6 | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 11267076 | openai/gpt-5.6-terra | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 1992633372 | openai/gpt-5.6-terra | error | HTTP 403 key limit |
+
+| G9 arm | Solved/attempts | Conclusion |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | unavailable |
+| hinted | 0/0 | unavailable |
+| placebo | 0/0 | unavailable |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo diagnostic is undefined until both arms run. The module records `0.0` only as a JSON-safe placeholder alongside `hinted_verdict="not_run"`; G9 correctly remains failed.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2004_11315 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+text = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>1, 2, 3</answer>")
+ok, reason = g.verify(inst, candidate)
+```
+
+From the repository root, after valid oracle evidence exists:
+
+```bash
+python3 results/2004.11315/gen_2004_11315.py
+scripts/emit.sh 2004.11315
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is a Track-B visual-compression benchmark. A sandboxed solver sorts the rows in milliseconds, and the measured reference success is expected. The `1/681,387,200` density applies only to the declared uniform prior over increasing triples; it says nothing about a solver that exploits repeated rows. The local panel does not include learned duplicate detection, suffix-array/text indexing, or a production graph-isomorphism system. The canonical key is a strong codegree/4-cycle invariant, not a complete bipartite isomorphism canon. Finally, the claimed 13-operation compact route counts work after the repeated row is recognized; whether model attention reliably supplies that recognition is exactly what the blocked oracle and G9 experiments still need to measure.
