401:\subsection{Path Compression}
402:We now show that a path of nodes with degree 2 can be eliminated together. More formally, let $P = \{a_1, a_2, \dotsc, a_k\}$ be a path in a graph $G = (V, E)$ with $\deg(a_i) = 2$ for all $a_i \in P$. There is a minimum fill-in ordering $\Sigma = x_1 \cdots x_i a_1 \cdots a_k x_{i+1} \cdots x_\ell$, where $V \setminus P = \{x_1,\dotsc,x_\ell\}$.
404:We prove this by distinguishing three cases based on which nodes are separation cliques, and using the relationship between minimum triangulations and minimum fill-in orderings. Corollary 1 and Proposition 2 from~\cite{rose72} are central to our proof and we restate them here.
411:        Let $C = (V, E)$ be a cycle with $|V| \geq 3$ nodes. Any ordering of $C$ is a minimum fill-in ordering.
413:Furthermore, we need to show that nodes with degree 2 in induced cycles of four or more nodes can be eliminated first.
418:To prove Lemma \ref{lem:deg-2-nodes} we establish that there exists a minimum triangulation that does not contain an edge to such a node $a$.
421:        Let $G$ and $a$ be as in Lemma \ref{lem:deg-2-nodes}. There exists a minimum triangulation $\hat{T}$ of $G$, with $\Gamma(a) \in \hat{T}$ and $\{a, x\} \notin \hat{T}$ for all $x \in V$.
424:        Let $\mathcal{C} = \{C_1,\dotsc,C_n\}$ be the set of induced cycles that contain $a$, i.e., for all $i$, $a \in C_i$ and $G[C_i]$ is a cycle. Since $a$ has degree two, $\Gamma(a) \subset C_i$ for all $i$.
425:        By Lemma \ref{lem:cycle}, for all $C_i \in \mathcal{C}$, there exists a minimum triangulation $T_i$ of $G[C_i]$ with $\Gamma(a) \in T_i$. Thus, there exists a minimum triangulation $\hat{T}$ of $G$ with $\Gamma(a) \in \hat{T}$.
426:        $\Gamma(a)$ is a separation clique with components $\{a\}$ and $V \setminus (\{a\} \cup \Gamma(a))$ in the triangulated graph $\hat{G} = (V, E \cup \hat{T})$. By Lemma \ref{lem:sep-clique} there exists no edge $\{a, x\} \in \hat{T}$.
